      "writtenOn": "2020-08-18T18:41:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks Diana!\n\nQuick q: Will the AP be notified of \"discovery complete\" via GOOG0003 (ACPI) notifier / EC_HOST_EVENT_PD_MCU (for non-ACPI), coupled with EC_CMD_PD_HOST_EVENT_STATUS?\n\nThat is what we currently use to send EC notifications to the Type C kernel driver, so it would be great if we can continue to use that (with possibly a new PD_EVENT for \"discovery complete\") ?",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "46e13e1f_1e34fa25",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-18T20:11:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Notifications are up next :) \n\nWe can talk about it next sync",
      "parentUuid": "882fb725_8bcce776",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "224bf25c_3be5d8de",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-19T20:19:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The discovery data structures currently aren\u0027t synchronized, which means that the command response could end up with inconsistent data under various circumstances. Do you anticipate that being a problem under the expected use of this command?",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "638ec9d3_5b6e78e9",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1181774
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-19T20:54:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We anticipate to call this command upon receiving a {SOP|SOP_PRIME}_DISCOVERY_DONE notification. For each port, do we expect the contents of the data structure to change once that notification is sent (of course, disconnects notwithstanding)? If not, then I think it should be fine.\n\nOne thing I noted was that the *_DISCOVERY_DONE will not tell us which port the discovery is completed for. AP will issue the EC_CMD_TYPEC_DISCOVERY for each port, so we will need some flag in the returned data structure to tell us that discovery is not complete for this port.",
      "parentUuid": "224bf25c_3be5d8de",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4b0e9066_3dbeff26",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-19T21:01:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "WRT synchronization,the only thing I can think of that would change the data structure while the command was running is if the port was detached or hard reset. I think we would also generate notifications for those, and the AP would eventually have a complete picture of the situation, but it could be a little messy.",
      "parentUuid": "638ec9d3_5b6e78e9",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f0437c2a_00d4d1e6",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-19T21:17:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Re: the notificaitons, can/should these be made port-specific?  It seems like it\u0027ll be a pain for the AP if it always needs to double-request information trying to figure out which port has events going on.\n\nRe: synchronization, Jett might be able to speak of the risk to copying data here mid-transition.  I always have to double-check myself with regard to which actions in a task can be preempted by other tasks.",
      "parentUuid": "4b0e9066_3dbeff26",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "51c0dba4_a75533dc",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1181774
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-19T21:26:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Re: the notificaitons, can/should these be made port-specific?  It seems like it\u0027ll be a pain for the AP if it always needs to double-request information trying to figure out which port has events going on.\n\nCan this be done in a facile fashion? Individual notifications for each port might start to get unwieldy as the number of ports increases.\n\nPerhaps another host command which returns a u8 bit mask for which ports the discovery is complete for (I guess two commands, one each for SOP and SOP\u0027)? Would this be moot if the limiting factor is IPC round trip time for each Host command?\nOpen to suggestions here..",
      "parentUuid": "f0437c2a_00d4d1e6",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c45d680a_d7bc1eca",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-19T22:06:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think the issue with notifications not being port-specific will be limited to discovery though, will it?  \n\nThings like soft resets, hard resets, initial connections, new source caps... all of these will be happening to just one port.  And detecting, for example, which port hard reset might be difficult after the fact unless we started tracking it somewhere for the AP to retrieve (ex. changing the current EC_CMD_PD_HOST_EVENT_STATUS to something stored by-port)",
      "parentUuid": "51c0dba4_a75533dc",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c76aa57a_64ff895c",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1181774
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-19T22:20:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Fair point.\n\n(Spitballing here):\n\nHow about extending the bitmask proposal to all notifications? I.e when the host receives an EC_CMD_PD_HOST_EVENT_STATUS bit field (e.g SOP_DISCOVERY_COMPLETE, or HARD_RESET), it sends an EC command (e.g EC_CMD_PD_NOTIFICATION_PORT_STATUS), in which the notification type (SOP_DISCOVERY_COMPLETE, HARD_RESET etc) can be the request argument, and what is returned is a bit mask for each port the notification applies to.\n\nOf course, I\u0027m only looking at this from the client/AP perspective, so I don\u0027t know how complex it is to implement on the EC side, but this makes it extendable to sizeof(bitmask) ports.\n\nI don\u0027t want to hijack this review CL any further, so we can continue the discussion in the notifications doc (https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-GojIqx-bsyeg4CCUkvdS6RUWC-aTAjxyyBKo8c96q4/edit) if you prefer.",
      "parentUuid": "c45d680a_d7bc1eca",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "79a7080a_ab14da32",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-08-19T22:32:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yup, we\u0027ll discuss that on the doc.\n\nBack to synchronization, we should synchronize here.  Jett confirmed that if an interrupt comes in, we could end up interrupted mid-memcpy and then task execution would go to the highest priority task (PD) before coming back to the host command task.",
      "parentUuid": "c76aa57a_64ff895c",
      "revId": "b4973d67fbf6d646f8435c652100cd26ebc33fac",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
